  "narrative": "Signed convention: for direction -1 claims, a positive signed estimate means the exposed group has a lower raw dependency_composite and therefore stronger measured dependency; for direction +1 claims, a positive signed estimate means a higher raw composite and therefore weaker measured dependency. The central dataset-level association map is now well established and independently validated: MSI-high and SMARCA4 loss associate strongly with stronger measured dependency; TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss associate strongly with weaker measured dependency; PTEN loss is a moderately accepted stronger-dependency association. The next scientifically valuable questions are residual-marker clarification, histologic/context robustness, and broad non-central predictors, not re-testing the large central axes. I activate H16 to test TP53 loss within adenocarcinoma, completing histologic robustness for TP53 alongside the already accepted squamous test, and I activate H44 to test the SMARCA4-loss by lineage interaction, which remains important for central-marker context independence. The new CDKN2A proposals target the main unresolved residual predictor. H73-derived clean TP53-intact and H74-derived clean TP53-loss comparisons remove the four other large marker axes plus PTEN, so they test whether CDKN2A loss has an independent stronger-dependency signal in either TP53 background. The expected direction is stronger dependency, but the scientific alternative is that CDKN2A is null, borderline, or only context-dependent. The CDKN2A low-9p21 proposal tests a genomic deletion-context hypothesis: if CDKN2A loss effects are most meaningful when 9p21 copy number is low, a clean-stratum comparison in low copy-number samples should show a threshold-level stronger-dependency difference. The alternative is no threshold-level effect or confounding by broad copy-number imbalance. The PTEN proposals refine the validated PTEN main effect into clean four-marker adenocarcinoma and squamous strata. Under robustness to other central markers and histology, both should show threshold-level stronger-dependency differences; if one histology is null, the PTEN main effect may be less context-independent. The RNA and DNA omics availability comparisons are technical/screening robustness checks. They are not presumed biological predictors; a threshold-level association would indicate that composite dependency scores vary with data-availability or batch-like features, which would matter for interpretation, while near-null effects would support that the main marker associations are not driven by these availability covariates. All comparisons remain dataset-level associations, not causal mechanisms or generalizable biological claims.",
  "research_notes": "Current accepted dataset-level associations: MSI-high and SMARCA4 loss -> stronger measured dependency; TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss -> weaker measured dependency; PTEN loss -> moderate stronger measured dependency. All five are independently validated. Histologic robustness has many accepted conditional tests; MSI-by-lineage interaction was rejected for the registered direction. Residual CDKN2A is the main unresolved predictor: broad and stratified estimates are near threshold and often imprecise. Active priorities: (1) H91 broad CDKN2A clean five-axis; (2) new clean CDKN2A splits by TP53 status; (3) new CDKN2A low-9p21 clean deletion-context test; (4) PTEN clean four-marker histologic refinements; (5) technical predictors H92 Avana library, H93 matched omics, new RNA/DNA omics availability, H94 sex; (6) constructed research-signature contexts H95-H98. H16 and H44 activated to finish histologic robustness for TP53 and SMARCA4. Validation budget remains but should be reserved for strong new discoveries or final targeted claims, because central axes are already validated."
